; KYX pins its own npm dependency policy, so the lockfile's shape does not
; depend on whoever happens to run the install.
;
; Why this file exists: the lockfile was generated on a machine whose USER-level
; .npmrc set `legacy-peer-deps=false`. With that flag npm does not enforce peer
; ranges, so the committed lock pinned `electron-builder-squirrel-windows@26.15.3`
; while `app-builder-lib@26.17.0` (pulled in by electron-builder) declares a peer
; dependency on exactly `26.17.0`. A plain `npm ci` - which is what CI runs, with
; no flags and no personal .npmrc - rejected that lock:
;
;   npm error code EUSAGE
;   npm error Invalid: lock file's electron-builder-squirrel-windows@26.15.3
;   npm error does not satisfy electron-builder-squirrel-windows@26.17.0
;
; and aborted in ~3 seconds, in every job, for 134 consecutive CI runs.
;
; A project-level .npmrc outranks the user-level one, so the policy below is what
; EVERY machine and CI use from now on. `npm run deps:check` proves the committed
; lock is still in sync under this exact policy before it reaches CI.
legacy-peer-deps=false
